  "headline": "Oil shock is being priced through yields, not havens: a stagflationary tilt",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as an energy-driven inflation scare with no growth impulse behind it. Brent is up 14% on the month and 81% on the year, and the 10-year Treasury yield has climbed 98 basis points year to date to 5.15%. Yet gold sits near the bottom of its range and copper is down on the month: markets are pricing a cost shock and a harder rate path, not expansion and not fear.",
    "The curve makes the case. The 30-year has risen less than the 10-year over the month, 14 basis points against 18, so the long end is bear-flattening — yields rising with the shorter maturity leading, which is the signature of inflation and policy pressure rather than a growth or term-premium story. Gold falling 5.8% on the month while yields rise says the same thing: higher real yields and a dollar up 1.3% on the month are overpowering any safe-haven demand.",
    "The reflationary tells are hollow. The copper/gold ratio is up 2.3% on the month and one bitcoin now buys 3.8% more gold, which would normally signal returning risk appetite, but both ratios are products of gold's slide rather than strength in the numerators — copper is down 3.6% on the month and bitcoin is down 2.2% in a calm volatility regime. The real-rate signal deserves the weight. This week's reprieve, with the dollar off 0.5% and yields down four to nine basis points, came while Brent rose another 4.5%, so the source of the pressure has not eased.",
    "The news explains the oil leg. MintPress News describes an IRGC strike strategy in the Strait of Hormuz, Drop Site News reports that Tehran believes a US-Israeli strike may come before the midterms and notes oil at a five-month high, and The Electronic Intifada reports Yemeni strikes on Saudi Arabia. The channel is Gulf supply and shipping, and crude has priced disruption risk at 86% of its 52-week range. But gold at 13% of its range and Brent's merely normal volatility say traders see an oil problem, not a systemic one. A strike of the kind Drop Site's source anticipates would be bullish for Brent, bearish for Treasuries as the inflation pass-through extends, and supportive for the dollar; gold would catch a haven bid, though the real-rate headwind has won all year.",
    "The currencies corroborate the oil read. USD/CAD is down 1.1% on the week as the loonie tracks crude. The outlier is the rupee: USD/INR is down 1.4% on the month despite India's import bill, so it is not trading as an oil-importer's currency and that firmness is worth watching if crude pushes higher."
  ],
